-Choose sections, ordering, titles, collapsed state and relevant views from the question and evidence, not a fixed report template. Every numeric claim must reference a deterministic fact; every selected view needs its own data observation, visual observation (null for spec-only), interpretation, limitation, next step and evidence. The compositor/validator is authoritative: repair the exact rejected fields without rerunning completed analysis. Pass the returned opaque `refs.dashboard_ref` to the sole writer as `{"dashboard": {"$dashboard_ref": "<refs.dashboard_ref>"}}` rather than copying dashboard JSON.
+Choose sections, ordering, titles, collapsed state and relevant views from the question and evidence, not a fixed report template. Every numeric claim must reference a deterministic fact. Each panel needs a plain-language observation, interpretation and limitation with evidence; per-view details, cross-chart context and next steps are optional, not repetitive required fields. Spec-only visual observations remain null, and all required evidence must still be inspected. The compositor/validator is authoritative: repair the exact rejected fields without rerunning completed analysis. Pass the returned opaque `refs.dashboard_ref` to the sole writer as `{"dashboard": {"$dashboard_ref": "<refs.dashboard_ref>"}}` rather than copying dashboard JSON.
 
 The `synthesis` object must contain exactly `format`, `report_title`, `thesis`, `thesis_evidence`, and `sections`; never include legacy top-level `purpose`, `title`, or `uid` inside it. Pass the new unique `uid` and dashboard `title` as separate `compose_ml_dashboard` arguments. Follow the current section schema rather than inventing report fields for audience metadata.
